7. THE SPIN-TEMPERATURE CAVEAT
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  T_s^-1 = (T_CMB^-1 + x_tot T_g^-1)/(1 + x_tot)  gives
      1 - T_CMB/T_s = [x_tot/(1+x_tot)] * (1 - T_CMB/T_g)
  The prefactor x_tot/(1+x_tot) is common to both runs and CANCELS in the
  ratio, PROVIDED x_tot is not itself a function of T_g.  At cosmic dawn the
  coupling is Lyman-alpha (Wouthuysen-Field), set by the stellar background,
  not by T_g -- so the ratio above holds at ANY coupling strength, and the
  registry's claim is the T_s -> T_g (strong-coupling) reading of it.
  The residue is the COLLISIONAL part x_c(T_g): a colder gas has a smaller
  collisional de-excitation rate, which pushes the ratio back toward 1.  At
  z ~ 17 collisional coupling is subdominant (x_c << x_alpha), so this is a
  de-rating of the number below, never a sign flip.

  amplification T_CMB/T_g / (T_CMB/T_g - 1) at z = 17  = 1.1623
  (i.e. a 1% colder gas gives a 1.162% deeper trough)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. REPRODUCING THE REGISTERED CRUDE CHAIN (does it give 4.6%?)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  registry chain: Gamma_C ~ 1/m_e^3  ->  -3 eps  ->  decoupling 3.8% earlier
                  ->  gas 3.8% colder  ->  trough 4.6% deeper

  step 1:  -3 eps = -3.7630 %   -> the registry's '3.8%' is exactly 3 eps.  OK.

  step 2:  '3.8% earlier decoupling' equates d ln Gamma_C directly with
           d ln(1+z_dec).  That skips the response slope.  Gamma_C/H ~
           (1+z)^4 x_e / (1+z)^{3/2}, so the true shift is
             d ln(1+z_dec) = 3 eps / |d ln(Gamma_C/H) / d ln(1+z)|.
           measured slope at z_dec = 123.957 :  d ln(Gamma_C/H)/d ln(1+z) = 2.7576
           => d ln(1+z_dec) = 3.7630% / 2.7576 = 1.3646 %  (not 3.76 %)
           integrated answer above: +1.3637 %

  step 3:  'gas 3.8% colder'.  After decoupling T_g ~ (1+z)^2/(1+z_dec), so a
           +d% shift in (1+z_dec) is a -d% shift in T_g -- IF decoupling were
           sharp.  It is not: residual Compton coupling below z_dec keeps
           re-heating the gas and erases part of the shift.
           integrated T_g shift at z = 17: -1.0055 % (rate only), -0.8792 % (self-cons.)

  step 4:  the depth amplification.  With x = T_CMB/T_g,
             ratio = (x(1+d) - 1)/(x - 1).
           registry's 3.8% with this run's T_g(17) = 6.852 K -> +4.417 % deeper
           to land on the registered 4.6% you need x = T_CMB/T_g = 5.750,
           i.e. T_g(17) = 8.532 K.  This run gets T_g(17) = 6.852 K (x = 7.160).

  VERDICT ON THE 4.6%: the amplification step is right (~1.16-1.21x); the
  chain's error is step 2+3 -- it charged the full 3 eps to the gas
  temperature.  The response slope divides it by ~2.8 and residual Compton
  coupling erodes it further.

